 ## 360° preview video
 
-Tick **Render 360° preview video** (off by default) to get a turntable MP4 (one rotation, 512 px per view, TRELLIS `render_utils.render_video` camera path) in the UI and as a download. **Video length (s)** sets the length, 4-15 s, default 12 s. Longer videos spin slower instead of rendering more frames: 10 frames per second of video, at most 120 (12 s = 120 frames at 10 fps, 4 s = 60 frames at 15 fps, 15 s = 120 frames at 8 fps), which keeps the VRAM and time cost at the old 4-second level. It is rendered straight after decoding, while the decoded model is still on the GPU, and shows up in the timings as *Render 360° video* (a few seconds). With Texture bake Fast or Quality the video shows the colour Gaussians and the mesh normals side by side (1024 × 512); with **None** the Gaussians are not decoded (that is what makes None fast), so the video shows mesh normals only (512 × 512). If the video fails, generation still finishes and the Export info box says so. Re-export does not change the video.
+Tick **Render 360° preview video** (off by default) to get a turntable MP4 (one rotation, 512 px per view, TRELLIS `render_utils.render_video` camera path) in the UI and as a download. **Video length (s)** sets the length, 4-15 s, default 12 s. Longer videos spin slower instead of rendering more frames: 10 frames per second of video, at most 120 (12 s = 120 frames at 10 fps, 4 s = 60 frames at 15 fps, 15 s = 120 frames at 8 fps). With Texture bake Fast or Quality the video shows the colour Gaussians and the mesh normals side by side (1024 × 512); with **None** the Gaussians are not decoded (that is what makes None fast), so the video shows mesh normals only (512 × 512). Re-export does not change the video.
 
-The video is protected against running out of VRAM (on Windows a full GPU does not fail, it spills into shared memory and crawls): the CUDA cache is emptied first; if less than 2.5 GB is free after decoding, the cached TRELLIS models are moved to system RAM for the rest of the run (they move back at the next generation, a few seconds); if VRAM is still short the video is reduced (half the frames at 384 px, or normals only at 256 px below 1 GB free; the length stays the same). Frames are rendered 10 at a time with the cache emptied in between, and the video is abandoned (the model still finishes) if it takes longer than 90 s or is on course to (set `IMG3D_VIDEO_TIMEOUT` to change the limit). Any of this is reported as a *Note* in the Export info box.
+**The meshes always come first.** The video is rendered after the GLB/OBJ/STL/PLY are exported: the downloads appear while the Export info box says *360° preview video: rendering ...*, and the video follows. Whatever happens to the video, the model is never lost.
+
+**VRAM / time guard** (on Windows a full GPU does not fail, it spills into shared memory and crawls; a 402 s run was seen at high settings before this guard):
+
+1. After decoding, the decoded mesh and Gaussians are moved to system RAM, so they hold no VRAM during hole filling and texture baking. Before the video, everything from the run is freed and the CUDA cache emptied, so only the cached TRELLIS models are on the GPU; if less than 2.5 GB is then free, those models are moved to system RAM for the video too (they move back at the next generation, a few seconds), and if VRAM is still short the video is reduced (half the frames at 384 px, or normals only at 256 px below 1 GB free; the length stays the same).
+2. Two test frames are rendered and timed. If the video would not finish within the time limit (90 s, `IMG3D_VIDEO_TIMEOUT` changes it), it is reduced further (fewer frames, 384 or 256 px, normals only); if even the smallest version would not fit, it is skipped straight away.
+3. Frames are rendered in small chunks (at most 10 frames or about 2 s each, sized from the latest measured speed) with the cache emptied in between, and the clock is checked after every chunk: the video is abandoned as soon as it passes the limit or is projected to. A single GPU call cannot be interrupted, so the limit can be exceeded by at most one chunk (about 2 s).
+
+Any of this is reported as a *Note* in the Export info box.
 
 ## Multi-image (experimental)
 
