+# Licence findings (2026-10-25)
+
+Short version for the owner. Details per package: [THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md](THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md). This is a technical audit, not legal advice; for the business question below a short check with a lawyer is worth it.
+
+## 1. Blockers
+
+**Two (three) components are research / non-commercial only, and two of them run on every generation:**
+
+| Component | Licence | When it runs |
+|---|---|---|
+| nvdiffrast (NVIDIA) | NVIDIA Source Code License "1-Way Commercial": use only "non-commercially", i.e. research or evaluation, "not for any direct or indirect monetary gain" | **Always** (TRELLIS hole filling rasterises 1000 views), plus Texture bake and the normals half of the video |
+| diff-gaussian-rasterization (Inria / MPII gaussian-splatting) | Research and evaluation only; commercial use needs Inria's prior consent (stip-sophia.transfert@inria.fr) | Texture bake Fast / Quality and the colour half of the 360° video. **Not** with Texture bake None and the video off |
+| diffoctreerast | Same Inria / MPII non-commercial terms | **Never** in this app (installed only because TRELLIS lists it) |
+
+Everything else, including all four model weights (TRELLIS MIT, DINOv2 Apache-2.0, BiRefNet MIT, SAM 2.1 Apache-2.0), is permissive or GPL/LGPL.
+
+## 2. A free public installer
+
+- **OK to publish** as long as it stays free and the README says clearly that nvdiffrast and diff-gaussian-rasterization are for research / evaluation / personal non-commercial use only, and ships their licence texts. Both licences allow redistribution if the complete licence text and notices go along (nvdiffrast §3.1, Inria §4.1).
+- Better: let the installer **download** these wheels (as `start.bat` does now) instead of bundling them, and show the restriction once during install.
+- **GPL / LGPL parts** (pymeshfix GPL-3.0, igraph GPL-2.0+, plyfile GPL-3.0+, easydict LGPL-3.0, the ffmpeg binary inside imageio-ffmpeg): free redistribution is fine; if the installer **bundles** them, include their licence texts and a pointer to their source (unmodified PyPI sources). The app's own MIT code is GPL-compatible, so no conflict. Not a blocker for a free installer; it would be for a closed-source paid product.
+- **Remove what is not needed** (fewer licences, smaller download): `diffoctreerast` (non-commercial, unused; TRELLIS only warns if it is missing), `gradio_litmodel3d` (no licence declared, unused), probably `transformers` (unused by the image pipeline). Test on the GPU PC before dropping them.
+- **sam2 from PyPI** is a third-party upload (not by Meta). Its code is identical to Meta's repository today, but for an installer pin Meta's GitHub source (`git+https://github.com/facebookresearch/sam2@<commit>`) or vendor the wheel you checked.
+- **plyfile is imported by TRELLIS but missing from requirements.txt** (and from the Windows lock): it must already be on the owner's PC from an earlier manual install. A clean installer needs it (GPL-3.0+; `pip install plyfile`).
+- Attribution: ship THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md and the licence files of nvdiffrast, gaussian-splatting, MeshFix/pymeshfix and igraph with the installer.
+
+## 3. Commercial use by Perth Resoles
+
+- Using the app to make models for the shop (e.g. shoe lasts for repairs or products you sell) is very likely "commercial" / "indirect monetary gain" under the nvdiffrast and Inria licences, **even if the app itself is free and only used in-house**. As the app stands, that use is not covered.
+- The **outputs** themselves are not restricted by the model licences (TRELLIS, DINOv2, BiRefNet, SAM 2 are all MIT/Apache), and nothing claims ownership of the meshes. The issue is running the non-commercial software for business, not the files.
+- Options, cheapest first:
+  1. Keep business use to evaluation / R&D until one of the options below is in place.
+  2. With **Texture bake: None** and the **360° video off**, diff-gaussian-rasterization is not run at all. nvdiffrast still is (hole filling), so this alone does not solve it.
+  3. Ask NVIDIA (nvdiffrast) and Inria (gaussian-splatting rasterizer) for a commercial licence; both offer them on request.
+  4. Engineering: replace the two rasterisers with permissive ones (e.g. a CPU/pytorch visibility test instead of nvdiffrast for hole filling, and Texture bake None so no Gaussian rendering). Several days of work and needs GPU testing; ask if you want it.
+- Not legal advice: a short review by an Australian IP/commercial lawyer before relying on it is recommended.
